@page "/chip"

<CatHead Title="Chips">
    A chip is a <em>thing</em> the user put there and can take away: an applied filter, a chosen
    recipient, a label attached to a record.
</CatHead>

<div class="callout callout--info">
    <i class="ri-question-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">Chip or badge?</span>
        Ask whether the user can remove it: if they put it there and
        can take it away it is a chip, and if it reflects data it is a <a href="/badge">badge</a>.
    </div>
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Chip.Tones" Title="Tones" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>A chip takes the same tones as a badge, for a filter that stands for a state or a category.</p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Chip.Chips" Title="Filter sets" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.chip-set</code> wraps a row of them onto a second line, and the remove button is a
        real <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> that needs a real name &mdash;
        <code>aria-label="Remove the region filter"</code>, never a bare <code>&times;</code>.
        <code>.chip--active</code> is a filter that is on rather than one offered, and
        <code>.chip-dot</code> has to be coloured from a semantic token in the markup.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Chip.Views" Title="Views at their own address" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A chip that is an <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> picks one of a few views that each have an address
        — the queues of a list, the areas of a long page — and wraps where a row of
        <a href="/tabs#strip-of-links">tabs</a> would scroll. <code>aria-current="page"</code> marks
        the view shown, and is its chosen look as well. Put the set in a <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code>
        with an <code>aria-label</code>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Chip.AppliedFilters" Title="Applied filters" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A chip's home: the <a href="/toolbar">toolbar</a> changes the filter, and the chip row
        below says what is currently applied and gives each one a way off. Name every remove
        button after the value it removes.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Chip.Recipients" Title="People in a field" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A chip can hold an <a href="/avatar"><code>.avatar</code></a> as well as a label;
        <code>.avatar--sm</code> is the size that fits inside the small tier, and
        <code>.avatar--muted</code> marks an actor that is not a person. The input goes after the
        chips, not inside them &mdash; a field whose value is a set of chips still needs one real
        labelled control for adding to it.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Chip.Sizes" Title="Two sizes" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A chip is interactive, so it takes the <a href="/concepts">control heights</a> rather than a
        type scale. The default is the small tier, for a filter bar beside a <code>.btn--sm</code>;
        <code>.chip--lg</code> is the normal tier, and <code>.chip--sm</code> a step under the default for a dense row or a table cell.
    </p>
</CatExample>
